09-09-2026 Build and Dependency Management with Maven

Maven and Gradle as common Dependency management platforms

Maven (Slide 3, Overview of What is)
    - Manages dependencies, builds, packaging, ang testing.
    - Build automation and dependency management tool for Java Projects

    - Project Object Model
    - Build Lifecycle
    - Plugin Engine

    - Output: Compiled classes, Test Reports, JAR/WAR

    - Always checks local repos first then falls back to remote repos

    - Local
    - Maven Central - default public repo, operated by Sonatype
    - Third-Party Repo (Google, JBoss)
    - Corporate/Private


Project Coordinates
    - groupId : artifactId : version : scope
    - eg com.example:my-app:1.0.0:compile
    - scopes: compile (Default), provided, runtime, test, system

Dependencies
    - Inputted into xml file, can exclude transitive dependencies with <exclusion></exclusion>
    - Direct vs Transitive, direct = you declare it, transitive = pulled in by a dependency you declared
    - sample command: mvn org.apache.maven.plugins:maven-dependency-plugin:3.11.0:add -Dgav=org.apache.commons:commons-lang3:3.17.0
    - Maven Conflict Resolver, nearest definition wins or use dependencyManagement

Plugins
    - extend Maven's build lifecycle, provide goals that compile code, run tests, package, generate reports
    - bound to a phase, Maven bounds to a lifecycle phase
    - During build, in phase order
    - eg maven-compiler-plugin, compiles java source code
    - live in the <build></build> section of the xml file

Build Profiles
    - dev, test, prod, switch build configuration for different env without editing the POM itself
    - each live within <profile> block inside of <profiles>
    - <id> and <properties>
    - confirm with mvn help:active-profiles

Lifecycle
    - mvn validate
        - Project structure and POM correctness
        - Everything required is declared
        - Repos and Settings are accessible
    - mvn compile
        - Compiles source code into bytecode
        - Processes resources
        - Reports compilation errors and warnings
        - Places compiled classes in output
    - mvn test
        - Executes unit tests (JUnit, TestNG)
        - Generates reports
        - Reports test failures
        - Stores test results
    - mvn package
        - bundles compiled coded and resources into a JAR/WAR and creates
        - includes manifest and metadata
        - places artifact in target dir
    - mvn verify
        - runs any verification goals bound in the POM (Integration tests, coverage checks, style checks, security scans)
        - ensures artifact is ready for installation
    - mvn install or mvn deploy
        - local vs remote

Dependency Scopes
    - Scopes define the purpose and visibility of a dependency across compile/test/runtime/packaged classpaths
    - compile, full lifecycle
    - provided, purely for testing
    - runtime, starts only on testing onwards, not required for compilation
    - test, only for test
    - system, not recommended, only up until runtime
    - <scope> within <dependency>

Transitive Dependencies
    - Resolved automatically and added to classpath
    - Maven handles version conflicts using the nearest definition wins rule
    - Don't need to declare explicitly
    - Declare only what your project directly needs

Reading Dependency tree
    - mvn dependency:tree
    - +- / \- mark a direct or last child; | shows a sibling.
    - each line ends with its scope (compile, test, runtime etc)

Resolving Conflicts
    - Maven picks version nearest to your POM
    - Override via a direct dependency
    - mvn dependency:tree (-DIncludes=..., -Dverbose shows every version considered)

DependencyManagement
    - declares a version without adding the dep to the build

